A||N||8||Monkeyman||Some lowering questions||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||06-14-2001||06:28 PM||||I just reshoed 4Play with some nice Yokohama Avids (225/50R15 on stock GT rims).  The lower profile tires look great.  Unfortunately, the 2.5 miles of wheel well space doesn't look as nice.  Good Lord, there's alot of wheel well!  Seems that I've spent most of my allowance on other Fiero related things, so I'm scraping the bottom of the bucket for spare change to lower the car.  What are some suggestions?  Two things to keep in mind.  One, I don't do any mechanical work myself and two, I don't have a ton of money to spend.  I don't think I can afford dropped spindles or coilovers (although I seem to remember someone selling some cheaper coilovers (rear only) that fit on stock struts?).  Would the ST springs be enough?  I don't plan on racing 4Play, but I definitely want the car to handle well.<P>BTW--I've done a search but can't seem to find the info I'm looking for.<P>MM||||reg||5
Z||000001||Greg Piet||06-14-2001||07:32 PM||||There is the cheap temporary fix till you can get do ti properly.<BR>There are the spring compressors which attach to the spring and essentially join up two coils together...  that will lower the car.<BR>I think the ride quality will stink and handling will be sub-par, but I think it might look more like what you want on the outside of the car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||06-14-2001||07:44 PM||||Thanx, but I'd rather not go that route.  I've heard that they can dig into the springs.  (Might not be a problem as I'm planning on getting new springs all around, but I can always sell the stock springs if they aren't damaged--have to recover some of the money spent on lowering.)<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||06-14-2001||09:04 PM||||put cinder blocks in the trunk and the front compartment- Cheap and effective plus has the added advantage of giving you a smooooth ride.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||fyerstarter||06-14-2001||09:33 PM||||Do not get spring compressors. They won't even lower it very much and it will ride really bad. <P>-The best and cheapest route would be ST lowering springs. $140 a set and most places will install for about $150. If you have basic mechanical skills and tools and rent a spring compressor from autozone you can do it yourself and save some money. <P>Also afterwards you will need to get a 4 wheel alignment, addition $50 approx. But you don't have to do this immediately. In fact it would be a good idea to let the springs settle for a few weeks before you do.<P>A few other alternatives:<P><BR>-Lowered spindles:<P>good: you will not get any negative camber which can affect steering and tire wear<P>bad: they are damn expensive and only lower the front.<P>-Cut your stock coils:<P>good: its cheap, all you need is a grinder and someone to take out and re-install the springs.<P>bad: since the stock coil rate is kept it will bottom out your shocks more often, this will cause damage to the shocks and also a bumpy ride. <P>If you do decide to go this route be sure and trim or remove your bumpstops.<P>-Heating Springs: <P>good: Really cheap, you don't have to remove the springs from the car, all you need is a torch and something to set under all 4 corners of your car so that when it drops it will be even.<P>bad: This is not a good idea, your ride will suck and the springs will become brittle from heating and can break.<P>-Coil Overs<P>good: Adjustable, and you can go really low<P>bad: expensive, and they only lower the rear<P><BR>Oh I've heard that if you want to lose just an inch on the rear you can get Chevy Citation front struts and put em on the back with the stock coils, or for an additional 1" drop with your lowering springs. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||c m west||06-14-2001||10:19 PM||||our loering spring will cure your problem, however the installer MUST modify the bumpstops by removing the bumpstops from the front, remove 3/4" from the bump stop mount, then reweld - as far as the rear - simply remove the bump stop plate, turn upside down, then reassymble ( on GT models it is neccassry to remove a microscopic portion from the engine louvers to clear the strut<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||06-15-2001||03:10 AM||||What about the Ground Control rear coil overs?  Are those a cheaper alternative to "regular" coil overs?  I already have a new set of struts (still in the boxes).  At $200, they don't seem to be a bad idea.  ST springs in the front?  Any problems with this combination?<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000007||fyerstarter||06-15-2001||12:16 PM||||That sounds like a good combo and probably what I will get for my GT.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ray b||06-15-2001||12:48 PM||||fill the gap with rubber trim strip off another car.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
